<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1169–c. 1229</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Geoffrey I of Achaea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin was a French knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> who helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-champlitte">William of Champlitte</a> conquer the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in 1205 and became Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> around 1209. He organised the principality on feudal lines under the nominal suzerainty of the Latin Empire, and his family ruled it through his sons Geoffrey II and William II. He was a nephew of the chronicler of the same name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin.jpg" alt="Geoffrey I of Villehardouin" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original design: unknown 13th-century artist; Sketch: Gustave Schlumberger (1844–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Geoffrey_I_of_Villehardouin_(Schlumberger,_1897).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
